{"step_index":565,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-04T06:48:30Z","input_tokens":3318,"cache_read_tokens":45308,"output_tokens":28372,"thinking":"The request involves unlocking the box and integrating functionality with the xview firmware. It's assumed that the xview firmware has been previously identified. Need to confirm and integrate the identified xview firmware unlocking process into the new functionality to unlock the box.\n\n\nThe \"Interfaz del Firmware del Xview Liberado\" refers to the liberated Xview firmware, specifically `DMC.v2.25` and `ViettelBox_B866V2`. This firmware includes boot logos, OEM settings, and configuration files like `Viettel_OTT_12`, extracted from the unlocked box. The TV interface is the Google TV/Android TV Launcher. The goal is to modify the program to achieve the desired result based on this understanding.\n\n\nThe liberated firmware, `DMC.v2.25`, includes several key applications, like the Google TV/Android TV Launcher, and a custom App Drawer (`AppDMC.apk`), App Store (`DLStore.apk`), HDPlay, File Manager, and Downloader. It also has a Button Mapper for the remote's home button and an AutoStart application. The `ControlDevice.apk` provides device control settings. Understanding the included apps is crucial for integrating the desired functionality.\n\n\nAnalyzing additional applications within the liberated firmware reveals several key components: \"Xuper Hydra,\" \"XSairec TV,\" \"MXL Movies,\" and \"MagisTV\".  A comprehensive list of packages associated with Megacable and ZTE locks has been identified for deactivation. Focusing now on configuring the liberated Xview interface as the default home activity for the TV.\n\n\nThe key is determining how the program can reliably launch the liberated Xview interface from any initial box state, without needing physical intervention. Initial boot logs show that booting into `fastbootd` via USB using the `RESET` button is possible, but the previous attempt faltered by trying to flash `env` and rebooting to bootloader. The `fastbootd` environment does not rely on the `env` variable. The approach must be modified to exploit `fastbootd`'s capabilities.\n\n\nTesting flashing various partitions directly in `fastbootd` (`PID_4EE7`) before booting. Investigating the timeout in the live test during `StartDedicatedWorldCupHuntFromLockedFastboot`. The timeout occurred because a `fastboot reboot` command was executed immediately without a countdown, preventing the user from holding the reset button for `fastbootd` boot. The function missed the conditions for `fastbootd` and Android boot modes in the loop.\n\n\nThe primary goal is the full liberation and transformation to the liberated Xview firmware (`DMC.v2.25`). The program needs to handle multiple connection methods: WorldCup, recovery fastbootd, U-Boot Fastboot, ADB USB, and ADB Red. The updated program will prepare the USB drive with necessary files to facilitate automatic execution of the core mission upon any connection type.\n\n\nThe program's primary objective is now a fully automated liberation process to the Xview firmware interface. This encompasses a unified intelligent engine capable of detecting the device in various states (WorldCup, Recovery Fastbootd, U-Boot Fastboot, ADB USB, ADB Red, or MTP) and transitioning it to the liberated firmware (`DMC.v2.25`). Phase A involves bootloader and partition liberation, addressing various device states and performing targeted flashing operations.\n\n\nThe process now identifies `U-Boot Fastboot` (`PID_0D02`). If the device is unlocked, liberated Xview firmware partitions are flashed, excluding `factory.img` and `cri_data.img`. When `U-Boot Fastboot` reports a locked device, an interactive sub-menu appears. The menu offers options to hunt for WorldCup unlock with a countdown and to reboot to Android for transformation via ADB/USB.\n\n\nFocusing on two core phases for the liberation: Phase A for bootloader/partition flashing, Phase B for full transformation to the liberated Xview interface. Phase A targets devices in `U-Boot Fastboot` and handles locked/unlocked states with specific flash operations. Phase B, triggered by ADB or menu selection, automates the final transformation.\n\nPhase B's steps now include injecting the `/cache/recovery/last_QR` to bypass the ZTE lock, unlocking OEM/U-Boot flags, and disabling Play Protect to ensure app installation. It also incorporates neutralizing Megacable and ZTE bloatware packages, uninstalling them to ensure a clean transition to the liberated firmware.\n\n\nIdentified specific packages for uninstallation to remove Megacable/ZTE bloatware: `com.megacable.updater`, `com.dotscreen.megacable.livetv`, `com.expway.ewm.service`, `com.zte.adb`, `com.ztestb.LauncherCustomize`, `com.zte.ums.oes`, `com.zte.ota`, `tv.upgrade.service`, `com.zte.tv.act`, `com.ztestb.uploaddiagno`, `com.zte.factorytesttool`, `com.hmdm.launcher`. Installation will include core Xview interface components from `2_APKS_LIBERACION_Y_TV`: Google TV/Android TV Launchers (`com.google.android.tvlauncher.apk`, `TVLauncher_4_GoogleTV.apk`, `Home_Screen_Launcher_1.1.3.apk`) and system applications such as `AppDMC.apk`, `DLStore.apk`, and `Filemanager.apk`.\n\n\nFocusing on ensuring the liberated Xview interface launches correctly and persistently. The process will set the liberated launcher (`com.google.android.tvlauncher`, `TVLauncher`, or `AppDMC`) as the default home activity. It also grants necessary permissions to the launcher and enables accessibility services to activate the button mapper for the remote control. The goal is to make the liberated interface the user's default experience after flashing.\n\n\nThe program now features automated one-click installation of the `DMC.v2.25` interface via ADB (USB/Network), along with options for direct firmware partition flashing while preserving Widevine L1 and MAC addresses. It also prepares a USB drive (Kingston or similar) with all necessary files, including keys and APKs for manual installation, and provides a catalog of individual firmware APKs.\n\n\nAutomatic local network ADB scanning is implemented, probing the local subnet for devices with ADB open on port 5555. This allows automatic device connection and full liberation, even without prior knowledge of the device's IP address, enhancing usability.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_file","args":{"Content":"using System;\nusing System.Collections.Concurrent;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing System.Diagnostics;\nusing System.IO;\nusing System.Linq;\nusing System.Management;\nusing System.Net;\nusing System.Net.NetworkInformation;\nusing System.Net.Sockets;\nusing System.Reflection;\nusing System.Text;\nusing System.Text.RegularExpressions;\nusing System.Threading;\nusing System.Threading.Tasks;\n\n[assembly: AssemblyTitle(\"PROGRAMA PERFECTO v3.0 - LIBERACION + INTERFAZ FIRMWARE XVIEW LIBERADO (B866V2)\")]\n[assembly: AssemblyDescription(\"Mision Principal: Liberacion Externa y Transformacion a Interfaz Firmware Xview Liberado DMC.v2.25\")]\n[assembly: AssemblyConfiguration(\"\")]\n[assembly: AssemblyCompany(\"Xview+ B866V2 Tools\")]\n[assembly: AssemblyProduct(\"PROGRAMA PERFECTO XVIEW+ B866V2 v3.0\")]\n[assembly: AssemblyCopyright(\"Copyright (C) 2026\")]\n[assembly: AssemblyVersion(\"3.0.0.0\")]\n[assembly: AssemblyFileVersion(\"3.0.0.0\")]\n\nnamespace ProgramaPerfectoB866V2\n{\n    public class ToolResult\n    {\n        public bool TimedOut { get; set; }\n        public int ExitCode { get; set; }\n        public string Output { get; set; }\n    }\n\n    public class BoxConnectionState\n    {\n        public string UsbHardwareMode { get; set; }\n        public string SerialNumber { get; set; }\n        public string RawPid { get; set; }\n        public bool FastbootLive { get; set; }\n        public List<string> AdbDevices { get; set; }\n        public List<string> FastbootDevices { get; set; }\n\n        pu